# Arc summary — spec 0033 (Materials & Library workbenches, structural samples, serializable dispersion values)

> **This file is NOT a substitute for stats.** It is the narrative companion to
> the numbers the stats tool computes over the impl-logs and
> `.manifest-arc-runner.log`; read the two together. (Provenance note: the
> supervisor session that babysat this arc crashed shortly after the arc
> completed, so this summary was reconstructed from that session's on-disk
> transcript and the arc artifacts rather than written from live memory.)

## How it went

The product work itself was remarkably quiet: all 26 slices completed on the
first judge cycle with a `done-green` verdict, zero route-backs, and no gate
ever failed on real code. Total wall time was ~14.5 hours across two runner
executions (2026-07-06 00:24–14:24 UTC for slices 001–024 plus a hibernate
gap, then 14:27–14:59 for the resumed 025–026), finishing with the finalise
commit `124d195` and a clean worktree. Every surprise of the arc was in the
harness around the slices, not in the slices.

**Surprise 1 — the arc refused to boot (exit 4).** The first launch died in
the health check: the manifest listed 26 slices but `.slices/` did not exist.
Root cause was an ordering gap left over from the v7 handoff cutover (direct
successor of the STL 0176 bug): handoff deliberately defers `.slices/`
emission to arc-runner boot, but the boot health check ran *before* the
step-compiler emit, so the first boot of any true at-rest v7 spec died on
exactly the files the compiler was about to write. Spec 0033 was the first to
hit it because 0176's slices had been rendered manually out-of-band. Fixed in
the ASG repo (a pre-emit step before the health check), verified against a
scratch copy of this spec, and documented in
`.manual/002-asg-error-boot-health-check-before-slice-emit.md`. The relaunch
booted clean and the fix held for the whole arc.

**Surprise 2 — a phantom Claude console window.** The detached runner
(`DETACHED_PROCESS`, from the 0176 kill-chain hardening) has no console, so
on Windows every console-app child it spawned got a brand-new *visible* —
and, because stdio is piped, completely empty — window. Cosmetic but
dangerous: closing that window with the X button would have killed the live
worker. Fixed in ASG (`CREATE_NO_WINDOW` at all nine headless spawn sites),
documented in `.manual/003-asg-error-visible-worker-window-under-detached-runner.md`,
and confirmed fixed in production when the resumed run showed zero windows.

**Surprise 3 — the machine hibernated ~2 hours mid-slice-025.** The worker
froze at 12:18 UTC and the runner sat silent beside it until the operator
stopped the run at ~14:24. Two lessons came out of the post-mortem. First,
the 1-hour hangup watchdog "failing to fire" was not a watchdog bug: Python's
monotonic timers do not advance during sleep, so after wake the runner
believed almost no idle time had passed (a wall-clock cross-check on resume
remains an open hardening candidate). Second, an earlier claim that nothing
could take the detached runner down was wrong — `taskkill /T` follows
parent-child links, so stopping the hosting shell is (and remains) the
correct way to stop the arc. The resume was routine: slice 025's retry
finished in 3.4 minutes, helped by the pre-hibernate partial work already in
the worktree.

**Surprise 4 — the monitor cried wolf on the final slice.** During slice 026
the arc monitor reported a stuck loop while the worker was demonstrably
healthy (its transcript was growing ~1 KB/s). The monitor was watching the
per-attempt console file, which legitimately sits at 0 bytes for a whole
worker phase, and it had no way to find the worker transcript because the
runner only logged that path at worker *exit*. Both gaps were fixed in ASG
after the arc completed (30-second worker heartbeats to the console stream,
plus a `WORKER-TRANSCRIPT` row at spawn that the monitor now tails) and
documented in `.manual/004-asg-error-monitor-blind-during-worker-phase.md`.

**Minor turbulence, absorbed silently:** two transient CLI failures were
retried by the runner's quota-retry path — slice 010's code judge (exit 1
after 10 s) and slice 021's implement worker (exit 1, retried ~40 minutes
later). Slice 021 was the only slice to need a second attempt task file, and
it still went done-green on cycle 1. No dead-ends were hit inside the product
work itself; nothing was tried and abandoned.

**Loose ends at time of writing:** the ASG monitor-gap fix (gap A/B,
`cli_transport.py` + `arc_monitor.py` + new tests) sat uncommitted on ASG
branch `0075`; the earlier no-window fix had already been committed there as
`a04cfdb`. The `.manual/004` note in this folder was untracked. None of this
touches the Berreman product code, which is fully committed on branch `0033`.
